Haemorrhoids
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Painless bright red rectal bleeding, Pruritus ani, Prolapsing anal tissue, Engorged venous cushions on anoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-029
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of exquisite, sharp pain near the anus with the sudden appearance of a hard, exquisitely tender lump, making sitting impossible.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, acute, and unrelenting perianal pain associated with a new palpable lump. Urgent out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was required to definitively rule out a perianal abscess (requiring emergency surgical drainage) versus an acutely thrombosed external haemorrhoid, and to provide immediate, targeted symptomatic relief prior to standard consulting hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "A hard, agonizing lump appeared near my back passage this morning after I strained on the toilet, and now I cannot sit down at all."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a history of intermittent painless bright red rectal bleeding seen as streaks on toilet paper over the past year. Notes chronic pruritus ani and a sensation of incomplete rectal emptying. Today, following an episode of heavy lifting and straining due to chronic constipation, patient developed exquisite, severe, and sharp perianal pain. Patient reports the sudden presence of a palpable, hard, very tender perianal lump. Denies fever, chills, or purulent discharge. Reports occasional prolapse of internal tissue through the anal canal during defecation requiring manual reduction in the past.
Functional Status: Patient is standing or lying on their side; totally unable to sit due to severe mechanical pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe pain severely limiting mobility and activities of daily living.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Patient appears highly uncomfortable, shifting weight constantly and refusing to sit.
Physical Exam: Visual inspection of the perianal area reveals a tense, distinct, 1.5 cm, bluish-purple, exquisitely tender subcutaneous nodule at the anal verge. No surrounding erythema, fluctuance, or induration to suggest an abscess. Digital rectal examination is deferred due to the severity of the pain and clear external pathology. The presentation strongly aligns with the history of painless bright red rectal bleeding, pruritus ani, prolapsing anal tissue, and implicitly engorged venous cushions, now complicated by an acute thrombosis.
Environmental Observations: Over-the-counter hydrocortisone creams visible in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Utilizing paracetamol with zero relief of the acute severe pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acutely Thrombosed External Haemorrhoid - Status: Exacerbated (Acute thrombosis)
Differentials:

Perianal Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of fever, the distinct bluish/purple appearance rather than diffuse erythema, and the acute hyperacute onset related to straining rather than a slowly progressive infectious swelling.

Anal Fissure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a discrete, tense, palpable external mass, rather than severe tearing pain specifically during defecation with an invisible or purely linear tear.

Prolapsed Rectum - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The mass being a discrete, eccentric, skin-covered venous lump rather than concentric rings of prolapsed rectal mucosa.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an exquisitely painful, acutely thrombosed external haemorrhoid, a severely debilitating but non-life-threatening condition. The acute onset of a severely painful perianal mass necessitated urgent out-of-hours evaluation to definitively rule out an acute perianal abscess or necrotizing infection, which would mandate emergency surgical intervention. Clinical examination confirmed a tense, thrombosed external venous cushion without signs of sepsis. Diagnosing this at the bedside prevented an unnecessary emergency department visit. The patient was provided with powerful topical analgesia, osmotic laxatives, and advice regarding non-operative management versus elective clot evacuation, safely managing the crisis overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Glyceryl Trinitrate (Rectogesic) 0.2% ointment to relax the sphincter and topical Lignocaine 5% ointment for direct analgesia. Prescribed Macrogol 3350 (Movicol) 2 sachets daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: Fill a shallow basin with warm water and soak your bottom for 15 minutes, three times a day. The warm water directly relaxes the severe spasm of the anal sphincter muscle that is clamping down on the clotted vessel, physically reducing the pressure and providing immediate, non-medicated pain relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Sitz bath basin.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48-72 hours. If pain remains completely intolerable, referral for minor surgical excision of the clot under local anaesthetic can be arranged.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you develop a high fever, if the lump begins to drain foul-smelling pus, or if you become completely unable to pass urine, present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clot will slowly resorb on its own over several weeks, but the skin may remain stretched as a painless skin tag.
Clinical Course: The pain typically peaks within the first 48 hours of the clot forming and then gradually subsides as the body begins to break down the trapped blood.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The lump is a trapped blood clot, not a dangerous infection. However, if the redness begins to spread rapidly across your buttocks, or if you develop severe fevers and shivering, you must go to the hospital to ensure it has not become infected.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Straining pushed a small blood vessel outside the anal opening, where the blood immediately clotted inside the vein. Because the skin there is extremely rich in nerve endings, this trapped, swollen clot is causing agonizing pain. The prescribed ointments will numb the nerve endings and relax the surrounding muscle ring to reduce the crushing pressure on the clot.
Actionable Steps: Do not push, squeeze, or attempt to 'pop' the lump under any circumstances. This will not release the clot but will tear the highly sensitive tissue, vastly increasing the pain and risking a severe bacterial infection from the stool.

SMS INFORMATION

Acutely thrombosed external haemorrhoid diagnosed. No abscess present. Scripts for numbing ointment and laxatives provided.

SMS EDUCATION

Use warm sitz baths 3 times daily. Apply ointment as directed. Take laxatives to avoid straining. Go to ED if high fever or pus develops.
